VI  - 34
DP  - 2021
TI  - Atypical carcinoid with multiple central airway metastases: A case report.
PG  - 101550
LID - 10.1016/j.rmcr.2021.101550 [doi]
LID - 101550
AB  - A 51-year-old man was found to have multiple polypoid tracheal and bilateral main 
      bronchial tumors during postoperative follow-up of atypical carcinoid. He 
      underwent transtracheal biopsy, and was diagnosed as having central airway 
      metastases of the atypical carcinoid. He underwent chemotherapy, but the effects 
      were unfavorable. Owing to the risk of airway obstruction, he was referred to our 
      hospital for interventional bronchoscopy. Carcinoid tumors usually present as 
      peripheral lung lesions or solitary endobronchial abnormalities, but rarely 
      appear as multiple central airway lesions. We present a very rare case of 
      multiple central airway metastases of atypical carcinoid, controlled by 
      bronchoscopic treatment.
